From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: maslow <wangfugen@126.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0800
Subject: Let long allocating builtins take interrupts, and throw instead of
 ending the process past the largest array

JSON.parse only checked for stack overflow: a termination requested while
it parses (a CPU or memory limit of the embedder) took effect only after
the whole text, however much it allocated meanwhile. Handle interrupts
once per value, as JSON.stringify does. String.prototype.split creates
every part in one runtime call; handle interrupts every 1,024 parts.
String.prototype.replaceAll with a string replacement builds a ConsString
per match without calling JavaScript; check the stack (and so interrupts)
per match. ValueSerializer checked for termination once per object only;
check before each string it flattens as well.

A JSON array or a split with more elements than FixedArray::kMaxLength,
and a JSON object with more properties than its dictionary can hold,
ended the process ("Fatal JavaScript invalid size error", "invalid array
length", "invalid table size"), reachable from a script with one text of
a few hundred megabytes. Throw a RangeError instead (Invalid array length,
Too many properties to enumerate); every element pushed counts, the first
one after the numbers' fast path as well, and with a reviver the limit is
half the largest array (its parse nodes take twice its length). The split
stops collecting indices one past the maximum.

Array.prototype.sort and toSorted sized their work array by the length
alone when the receiver had no own elements (an array-like object, a
string, a proxy): a length past the largest array ended the process
before an element was read. Start such a work array at 1,024 entries and
let it grow with the elements found (Torque's GrowableFixedArray now
stops at the largest array and throws a RangeError when that is full,
instead of ending the process); collecting them visits every index up to
the length, so handle interrupts once per 1,024 indices there. A global
regexp replace with a function collects every match first: handle
interrupts every 1,024 matches, throw a RangeError before the builder
outgrows the largest array. A global atom match throws a RangeError when
it counts more matches than the largest array holds.

